"""Independent Hookean teaching in the mathematical order of book 16.1–16.3."""
import sympy as s
lessons=[];models=[]
def T(v):return s.latex(s.sympify(v))
def D(*rows):return r'\[\begin{gathered}'+r'\\'.join(rows)+r'\end{gathered}\]'
def E(code,title,question,solution,kind,**data):
 uid='MHOOK-E-'+code;models.append(dict(id=uid,kind=kind,**data));return dict(title=title,question=question,solution=solution,verification=uid)
def L(key,title,summary,objective,before,ideas,method,mistakes,examples):
 lessons.append(dict(key=key,id='fm_hooke_'+key,title=title,summary=summary,objective=objective,before=before,ideas=ideas,method='<ol>'+''.join('<li>'+m+'</li>' for m in method)+'</ol>',mistakes=mistakes,examples=examples))
base='/courses/maths4u-9231-mechanics/topics/fm_hooke_'
L('models','Elastic models, equilibrium and changes of load','Distinguish natural length, extension, modulus and the actual resultant force.',
 'Build a correct Hookean force model and interpret an equilibrium or a change of load.',
 '<p>Review resolving forces and the <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-mechanics/topics/fm_equilibrium_contacts">contact-force lesson</a>. Numerical examples use g=10 m s⁻² where stated. For the energy parts of the written load-change questions, continue with <a href="'+base+'energy">elastic energy</a> before attempting those parts.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Length and extension are different measurements</h3><p>Let ℓ₀ be natural length, ℓ the current end separation and x=ℓ−ℓ₀ the signed extension. For a light Hookean spring kept straight, the restoring force has magnitude λ|x|/ℓ₀, where λ is its modulus. It pulls when extended and pushes when compressed. The signed force opposes the displacement from natural length.</p>'''+D(r'k_s=\frac{\lambda}{\ell_0}',r'F_{\rm restoring}=-k_s x')+r'''<p>Modulus λ has units N, while stiffness kₛ has units N m⁻¹. They are not interchangeable. Cutting a uniform string into two equal material parts retains the same modulus but halves each natural length, so each part has twice the original stiffness.</p><h3>An elastic string cannot push</h3>'''+D(r'T=\begin{cases}\dfrac{\lambda(\ell-\ell_0)}{\ell_0},&\ell\ge\ell_0,\\0,&\ell\lt\ell_0.\end{cases}')+r'''<p>A string shorter than its natural length is slack. Do not extend the tension formula to a negative force. A guided spring can instead remain straight during compression. State which object and guide the question specifies; they can lead to different turning points.</p><h3>Equilibrium is a force condition</h3><p>A hanging particle in vertical equilibrium has T=mg, giving extension mgℓ₀/λ. On a smooth slope, resolve the weight along the slope. Under a horizontal holding force as well as weight, the tension is the resultant magnitude and its direction follows from the force components. Do not put T=mg for every sloping string.</p><h3>Changing the load does not instantly change the length</h3><p>If a load is attached while the particle is held and then released, calculate the new equilibrium using the new moving mass. The release position is the old length, not the new equilibrium length. Removing a load without an impulse leaves the remaining particle's instantaneous velocity unchanged, but its acceleration generally changes.</p><h3>A useful energy preview</h3><p>For a straight smooth hanging spring, measuring signed displacement q downwards from its new equilibrium completes the square in the total potential. If released from rest at q=D,</p>'''+D(r'\frac12mv^2+\frac12k_s q^2=\frac12k_s D^2')+r'''<p>This is an energy identity, derived in the next lesson. It is valid only while that same spring model remains valid. For a string, check that the entire proposed path stays stretched; otherwise use a separate slack stage.</p><h3>Circular motion is a separate force application</h3><p>For an elastic conical pendulum, first use vertical and radial components, then Hooke's law to find length. The actual orbit radius is ℓ sinθ. The <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-mechanics/topics/fm_circular_conical">conical-pendulum lesson</a> gives the derivation. Non-degenerate motion excludes zero radius, which can make the lower endpoint of an angular-speed interval strict.</p>''',
 ['Choose spring or tension-only string, and identify its actual natural length.','Draw forces and use the correct signed extension in Hooke’s law.','For equilibrium, resolve forces before finding length or modulus.','After a load change, distinguish the old release position from the new equilibrium.','Check extension, guide/contact conditions, units and any strict geometric bounds.'],
 ['Use extension, not total length, in the force law.','Modulus is a force; stiffness is force per unit length.','A shortened string is slack, not compressed.','Removing mass changes the force balance even without an impulse.','A nonzero circular orbit excludes the vertically hanging limiting state.'],[
 E('M1','1 · Calibrate an unknown natural length',r'<p>A hanging spring has length 1.3 m under a 10 N load and 1.9 m under a 30 N load. The same Hookean law holds in both trials. Find its stiffness, natural length and modulus.</p>',D(r'k_s=\frac{30-10}{1.9-1.3}=\frac{100}3',r'\ell_0=\frac{13}{10}-\frac{10}{100/3}=1',r'\lambda=k_s\ell_0=\frac{100}3')+r'<p>The stiffness is \(\frac{100}3\) N m⁻¹ and the modulus is \(\frac{100}3\) N. Their equal numerical values here result from the 1 m natural length, not from equal units.</p>','calibration',forces=[10,30],lengths=['13/10','19/10'],stiffness='100/3',natural=1,modulus='100/3'),
 E('M2','2 · A horizontal force changes the tension',r'<p>A particle of weight 15 N is held by an 8 N horizontal force on a string of natural length \(\frac65\) m and modulus 68 N, attached to a ceiling point. Find the tension and vertical depth below the ceiling.</p>',D(r'T=\sqrt{15^2+8^2}=17',r'e=\frac{17}{68}\cdot\frac65=\frac3{10}',r'\ell=\frac32',r'\cos\theta=\frac{15}{17}',r'h=\frac32\frac{15}{17}=\frac{45}{34}')+'<p>Both force components belong to the same tension. The vertical depth is the stretched length times the vertical direction cosine.</p>','holding',weight=15,horizontal=8,natural='6/5',modulus=68,tension=17,depth='45/34'),
 E('M3','3 · A guided spring can supply thrust',r'<p>A spring stands vertically on a fixed base and is kept straight by a smooth guide. Its natural length is \(2a\) and modulus \(8mg\). A mass m rests on its upper end. Find its equilibrium compression and length.</p>',D(r'\frac{8mg}{2a}c=mg',r'c=\frac a4',r'\ell=2a-c=\frac74a')+'<p>The spring pushes upwards when compressed. A tension-only string could not support this configuration in the same way.</p>','compression_equilibrium',natural=2,modulus=8,compression='1/4',length='7/4'),
 E('M4','4 · Attach a load without an impact',r'<p>A mass m hangs at rest from a string of natural length a and modulus \(6mg\). While held at that length, another mass m is attached. The combined mass is released from rest. Find its greatest speed and greatest extension.</p>',D(r'e_0=\frac a6,\qquad e_{\rm eq}=\frac a3',r'\frac12(2m)v_{\max}^2=\frac12\frac{6mg}{a}\left(\frac a6\right)^2',r'v_{\max}=\sqrt{\frac{ag}{12}}',r'e_{\max}=2e_{\rm eq}-e_0=\frac a2')+'<p>All proposed extensions are positive, so the string stays taut. There was no collision: energy is applied only after release, with the combined mass 2m.</p>','load_add',oldMass=1,newMass=2,modulus=6,start='1/6',equilibrium='1/3',speed2='1/12',maximum='1/2')])
L('energy','Elastic energy, extension and compression','Derive stored energy and combine it with gravity and kinetic energy over a valid stage.',
 'Use the work–energy principle to find speeds, extrema and physically admissible turning points.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'models">elastic force models</a>. Review kinetic energy, gravitational potential energy and solving quadratics. A fixed smooth guide constrains direction but does no work along the allowed path.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Stored energy is the work of a variable force</h3><p>Increasing a spring extension slowly from zero to x requires work equal to the area under the force–extension graph. The graph is a straight line through the origin, so the area is a triangle. Equivalently, integrate the force:</p>'''+D(r'U_e(x)=\int_0^x\frac{\lambda s}{\ell_0}\,ds',r'U_e=\frac{\lambda x^2}{2\ell_0}=\frac12k_sx^2')+r'''<p>For a guided spring this is also positive during compression: signed x is negative but x² is positive. The spring's work between two states is the decrease of this energy. Force times final extension would give twice the stored energy, not the correct work.</p><h3>A slack string stores no elastic energy</h3>'''+D(r'U_e(\ell)=\frac{\lambda}{2\ell_0}\big(\max(\ell-\ell_0,0)\big)^2')+r'''<p>Use current separation to decide whether the string is taut. Crossing natural length does not necessarily stop the particle. Its tension and elastic energy then become zero, while speed remains continuous in an ideal non-impulsive transition.</p><h3>Write one consistent energy balance</h3>'''+D(r'K_i+V_i+W_{\rm applied}=K_f+V_f+W_{\rm lost}',r'K=\frac12mv^2,\qquad V=mg y+\sum U_e')+r'''<p>Use upward height y for gravitational potential. Work lost to sliding friction is positive in the loss term. When travelling up a slope, the height rise is distance times the sine of inclination. Energy depends on speed squared; it does not tell you the direction of velocity without the stated motion.</p><h3>Turning points require a reachable root</h3><p>At a turning point v=0. One root often reproduces the starting point, so select the next point in the direction of travel. Check the force model and geometry on the intervening path. For a vertical spring, use signed extension x downwards from natural length:</p>'''+D(r'V(x)=\frac12k_sx^2-mgx',r'x_{\rm eq}=\frac{mg}{k_s}',r'V(x)=\frac12k_s(x-x_{\rm eq})^2-\frac{(mg)^2}{2k_s}')+r'''<p>Equal-potential turning points of this same spring stage lie equally far from equilibrium. This energy observation needs no time-dependent oscillation formula. It cannot be used through a slack-string interval as if the string were a compressible spring.</p><h3>Maximum speed is minimum reachable potential</h3><p>On a smooth stage, speed is greatest where potential is smallest along the actual allowed path. Check force balance at an interior candidate and compare any boundaries. In a multi-stage problem, compare candidates from every stage. Use force balance again for acceleration; a large speed does not imply a large acceleration.</p>''',
 ['State the coordinate, natural length and active elastic branches.','Write kinetic, gravitational and every elastic energy with one reference.','Include applied work and friction losses with consistent signs.','Solve for every requested speed or turning point, rejecting unreachable roots.','Check maximum-speed candidates and obtain acceleration from the actual resultant.'],
 ['Stored energy is half force times extension for a Hookean element.','Compression has positive stored energy, not negative energy.','A string has zero energy and force while slack.','The old release point is not the next turning point.','Maximum speed and maximum spring force generally occur at different positions.'],[
 E('E1','1 · Work is an energy difference',r'<p>A spring has natural length \(\frac45\) m and modulus 40 N. Find its force magnitude and stored energy at extension \(\frac3{10}\) m, and the work needed to increase extension from \(\frac1{10}\) m to \(\frac3{10}\) m.</p>',D(r'k_s=\frac{40}{4/5}=50',r'T=50\left(\frac3{10}\right)=15',r'U_e=\frac12(50)\left(\frac3{10}\right)^2=\frac94',r'W=U_e(3/10)-U_e(1/10)',r'W=\frac12(50)\left(\frac9{100}-\frac1{100}\right)=2')+'<p>The force is 15 N, the stored energy is 9/4 J and the work over the stated interval is 2 J. The final force is not constant during the extension.</p>','energy_work',natural='4/5',modulus=40,start='1/10',end='3/10',force=15,energy='9/4',work=2),
 E('E2','2 · The next stop can involve compression',r'<p>A guided vertical spring of natural length a and modulus \(6mg\) hangs from a fixed point and carries a mass m. It is released from rest at length \(\frac32a\). Find the next turning length and greatest speed.</p>',D(r'x_{\rm eq}=\frac a6,\qquad x_0=\frac a2',r'x_1=2x_{\rm eq}-x_0=-\frac a6',r'\ell_{\min}=a+x_1=\frac56a',r'v_{\max}^2=\frac{6g}{a}\left(\frac a3\right)^2=\frac23ag')+'<p>The spring is compressed at the next stop. This is permitted by its guide. Replacing it by a string would change the motion after natural length was crossed.</p>','vertical_spring',modulus=6,start='1/2',turn='-1/6',length='5/6',speed2='2/3'),
 E('E3','3 · Choose the physical quadratic root',r'<p>A string has natural length a and modulus \(5mg\). A particle of mass m is projected down a smooth slope from natural length with speed \(\sqrt{\frac25ag}\). The inclination has sine \(\frac35\). Find its greatest extension.</p>',D(r'\frac15mga+\frac35mgx=\frac{5mg}{2a}x^2',r'z=\frac xa,\qquad25z^2-6z-2=0',r'z=\frac{3\pm\sqrt{59}}{25}',r'x=\frac{3+\sqrt{59}}{25}a')+'<p>The negative algebraic root is outside this stretched-string stage. Potential decreases first and then increases, so the positive root is the first stop on the descent.</p>','incline_launch',modulus=5,sine='3/5',initial2='2/5',extension='(3+sqrt(59))/25'),
 E('E4','4 · Recover a modulus from two moving states',r'<p>On a smooth horizontal guide, a mass m moves away from a fixed end. Its string has natural length a. Speed is \(\sqrt{3ag}\) at length \(\frac54a\), and \(\sqrt{2ag}\) at length \(\frac32a\). Find the modulus and the tension at the second point.</p>',D(r'\Delta K=\frac12m(3ag-2ag)=\frac12mga',r'\Delta U_e=\frac{\lambda}{2a}\left(\frac{a^2}4-\frac{a^2}{16}\right)',r'\Delta K=\Delta U_e',r'\lambda=\frac{16}3mg',r'T=\lambda\frac{a/2}{a}=\frac83mg')+'<p>Both states are stretched and are in the same smooth stage. The increase in elastic energy equals the decrease in kinetic energy.</p>','speed_measurements',firstExtension='1/4',secondExtension='1/2',first2=3,second2=2,modulus='16/3',tension='8/3')])
L('systems','Coupled elements, geometry and friction','Sum all elastic energies, then test the geometry and the friction required at rest.',
 'Solve linked elastic systems with correct half-string properties, separate forces and work losses.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'models">Hookean force models</a> and <a href="'+base+'energy">work and stored energy</a>. Review <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-mechanics/topics/fm_equilibrium_contacts">sliding and static friction</a>. Read every member’s natural length and modulus separately.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Each element has its own extension</h3><p>For a particle between anchors A and B separated by D, let q=AP. The member lengths are q and D−q. Tensions can be equal on a horizontal table in equilibrium, but a vertical system needs their difference to balance weight. A spring may push; a slack string contributes zero.</p>'''+D(r'U_e=\sum_i\frac{\lambda_i x_i^2}{2\ell_{0i}}')+r'''<p>The displayed sum is for active springs or stretched strings. Use the positive-extension branch for strings. Write the force law before solving and check that every assumed taut member has positive extension.</p><h3>A midpoint attachment halves natural length, not modulus</h3><p>For a uniform string fixed at its midpoint to a particle, each material half keeps the original modulus but has half the original natural length. In a symmetric geometry the half-length is found by Pythagoras. Add the energies of both halves; do not count only one. Equal tensions can cancel at a collinear position despite nonzero stored energy.</p><h3>Symmetric does not mean that tension equals weight</h3><p>For a particle a distance y below the mid-level of equal supports at horizontal half-separation b, each half-string has length L=√(b²+y²). Vertical equilibrium requires</p>'''+D(r'2T\frac yL=mg')+r'''<p>After release, replace vertical balance by Newton’s second law or use the energy of both halves with gravity. An ideal rigid rod or smooth guide can constrain the path without doing work, but can still have a nonzero reaction. A holding force removed before motion must not be included in the later energy balance.</p><h3>Sliding friction dissipates energy</h3>'''+D(r'W_{\rm lost}=\int\mu N\,ds')+r'''<p>On a horizontal table with only weight and vertical normal, N=mg and the loss is μmg times distance travelled. On a slope with no other normal components, N=mg cosα. If a string or applied force changes the normal, calculate N first. Friction reverses when motion reverses.</p><h3>A first stop is not necessarily permanent rest</h3><p>At a stopping point, recompute the friction needed for static equilibrium. It may be smaller than its maximum and may point in a different direction from the preceding sliding friction. Permanent rest is possible only if the required magnitude is at most μN. A nonzero spring force does not by itself imply that motion restarts.</p><h3>Check the whole path</h3><p>A both-taut quadratic energy model may cease to apply before the next stop. Identify natural-length boundaries and switch the relevant term off. Conversely, a guided spring remains energetic through compression. Geometric symmetry is a constraint to verify, not permission to ignore one of the forces.</p>''',
 ['Express all lengths from one coordinate and calculate each actual extension.','Resolve forces for equilibrium or an instantaneous acceleration.','Add every active elastic energy and the gravitational term.','Subtract work lost along the travelled path, splitting branches when needed.','At each stop check static friction, tautness, geometry and the next possible event.'],
 ['Equal modulus does not mean equal stiffness for different natural lengths.','Each half of a uniform string has the same modulus as the whole material.','Include both half-string energies in a symmetric system.','Sliding friction and the friction required for final rest need not be equal.','A both-taut formula is valid only while both extensions remain non-negative.'],[
 E('C1','1 · Two springs share one displacement',r'<p>A mass m lies between anchors \(5a\) apart on a smooth horizontal guide. The left spring has natural length a and modulus \(2mg\); the right has natural length \(2a\) and modulus \(4mg\). Find equilibrium and the greatest speed after a displacement \(\frac a4\) from it and release from rest.</p>',D(r'z=\frac qa',r'2(z-1)=2(3-z)',r'q_{\rm eq}=2a,\qquad T=2mg',r'k_{\rm total}=\frac{4mg}{a}',r'\frac12mv_{\max}^2=\frac12\frac{4mg}{a}\left(\frac a4\right)^2',r'v_{\max}=\frac12\sqrt{ag}')+'<p>Both springs have positive extension throughout this small motion. The greatest speed occurs at the minimum of their combined potential, not where either spring alone has natural length.</p>','two_springs',distance=5,leftNatural=1,rightNatural=2,leftModulus=2,rightModulus=4,displacement='1/4',equilibrium=2,tension=2,speed2='1/4'),
 E('C2','2 · A vertical system needs a difference of tensions',r'<p>Anchors A above B are \(10a\) apart. A particle of mass km lies between them. The upper string has natural length \(2a\) and modulus \(4mg\); the lower has natural length \(3a\) and modulus \(3mg\). Find BP in equilibrium and the full k-range for both strings to be stretched. For k=1, give both tensions.</p>',D(r'z=\frac{BP}{a}',r'\frac{T_U}{mg}=2(8-z),\qquad\frac{T_L}{mg}=z-3',r'2(8-z)-(z-3)=k',r'BP=\frac{19-k}{3}a',r'3\lt z\lt8\quad\Longrightarrow\quad0\lt k\lt10',r'k=1:\qquad T_U=4mg,\quad T_L=3mg')+'<p>The lower string pulls downwards, along with weight. Both-taut endpoints are excluded because an extension becomes zero there; k is positive by definition.</p>','vertical_pair',distance=10,upperNatural=2,lowerNatural=3,upperModulus=4,lowerModulus=3,position='(19-k)/3',range=['0','10'],tensions=[4,3]),
 E('C3','3 · Sliding stops before the string becomes slack',r'<p>A mass m on a rough horizontal table is joined to a string of natural length a and modulus \(4mg\). It is released at extension \(\frac a2\). Sliding and limiting static coefficients are both \(\frac32\). Find its extension at the first stop and decide whether it stays there.</p>',D(r'T_0=2mg\gt\frac32mg',r'z=\frac xa',r'2\left(\frac14-z^2\right)=\frac32\left(\frac12-z\right)',r'x=\frac a2\quad\text{or}\quad x=\frac a4',r'T_{\rm stop}=mg\lt\frac32mg')+'<p>The second root is the next stop; the release root must be discarded. The final friction required is mg outwards, below its allowed maximum, so the particle remains at rest with the string stretched. A coefficient greater than one is physically permitted in this ideal model.</p>','friction_stop',modulus=4,start='1/2',mu='3/2',stop='1/4',staticRequired=1),
 E('C4','4 · Two half-strings and a vertical launch',r'<p>Equal supports are \(6a\) apart. A uniform elastic string of natural length \(4a\) is fixed between them with a mass m attached to its material midpoint. It hangs \(4a\) below the support level in equilibrium. Find its modulus and stored elastic energy. If launched vertically upwards from equilibrium at speed \(\sqrt{6ag}\), find its speed when it first reaches support level.</p>',D(r'L_{\rm eq}=5a,\qquad \ell_{0,\rm half}=2a',r'2T\frac45=mg,\qquad T=\frac58mg',r'\lambda=T\frac{2a}{3a}=\frac5{12}mg',r'U_{\rm eq}=2\frac{\lambda(3a)^2}{4a}=\frac{15}8mga',r'U_M=2\frac{\lambda a^2}{4a}=\frac5{24}mga',r'\frac{v_M^2}{2ag}=3+\frac{15}8-4-\frac5{24}',r'v_M^2=\frac43ag')+'<p>Each half retains modulus λ. Potential increases towards support level during this ascent, so positive speed there rules out an earlier stop. The equal tensions are horizontal and opposite at that instant.</p>','symmetric_launch',halfNatural=2,halfSpan=3,equilibriumDepth=4,initial2=6,modulus='5/12',energy='15/8',final2='4/3')])
L('stages','Slack phases, collisions and changes of force','Use a new model at natural length, an impact or the boundary of a rough region.',
 'Track position and speed between valid elastic stages and select the first reachable event.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'energy">elastic energy</a> and <a href="'+base+'systems">coupled systems and friction</a>. Direct collisions use signed momentum and restitution, as explained in <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-mechanics/topics/fm_circular_stages">the circular-motion stages lesson</a>. This lesson uses energy and force balance, without time-dependent oscillation formulas.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Natural length is a model boundary for a string</h3><p>At ℓ=ℓ₀ the tension and elastic energy are zero. If motion continues to shorter separation, use only the forces still active. Under vertical gravity a free particle can continue upwards, slowing until its speed is zero. Position and speed are continuous at an ideal slackening transition; do not remove its remaining kinetic energy.</p><h3>Passing the fixed end changes geometry</h3><p>If the question expressly allows passage through an ideal attachment point, let q be a signed coordinate along its guide. String separation is |q|, not q. The string is slack for |q|&lt;ℓ₀, then can stretch on the other side. Its pull is always towards the fixed end.</p>'''+D(r'U_e(q)=\frac{\lambda}{2\ell_0}\big(\max(|q|-\ell_0,0)\big)^2')+r'''<p>Include the gravitational term with the sign of the chosen coordinate. Evaluate maximum-speed candidates on each active branch and at the boundaries. Never turn a negative coordinate automatically into a negative extension.</p><h3>An impact is not a smooth energy stage</h3><p>For two particles that stick together, conserve momentum along the impact line if there is no external impulse in that direction:</p>'''+D(r'm_1u_1+m_2u_2=(m_1+m_2)V')+r'''<p>Use the total moving mass afterwards, and subtract the collision energy loss before the subsequent smooth spring motion. A finite spring force does not supply an instantaneous impulse during an ideal direct collision. Adding a stationary load while holding a position is a different process: no impact velocity calculation is required.</p><h3>A force discontinuity need not cause a velocity jump</h3><p>At the edge of a rough strip with no step or impact, speed remains continuous. Friction switches on and the acceleration changes immediately. Calculate the smooth-side and rough-side resultants separately. Continue energy with friction work only over the rough distance travelled.</p><h3>Use a timeline and test all event candidates</h3><p>Label the release, each natural-length crossing, each collision, the support point and the next stop. State which event happens first and which energy terms survive. If an energy equation gives two positions with the same speed, choose the one encountered first in the stated direction. Check that no earlier zero speed or invalid contact interrupts the path.</p>''',
 ['Locate the first boundary of the current physical model.','Carry position and speed continuously through a non-impulsive transition.','At a collision use impulse laws, then restart energy with the new moving mass.','Activate only the forces and energies valid on the new stage.','Check first-event order, both coordinate signs, static friction and all requested extrema.'],
 ['A slack string has neither compressive force nor compressive energy.','Use absolute separation for a string when the particle passes its fixed end.','Do not conserve kinetic energy across a coalescing collision.','At a rough boundary speed can be continuous while acceleration jumps.','Select the first equal-speed passage, not merely any algebraic root.'],[
 E('S1','1 · Rise continues after tension disappears',r'<p>A string of natural length a and modulus \(4mg\) supports masses m and 2m together in equilibrium. The 2m load is removed without an impulse. For the remaining particle, find the speed at natural length, least length in its first ascent and initial acceleration.</p>',D(r'e_0=\frac{3a}4,\qquad U_0=\frac98mga',r'\frac12mv_N^2=\frac98mga-\frac34mga=\frac38mga',r'v_N^2=\frac34ag',r'\Delta h_{\rm slack}=\frac{v_N^2}{2g}=\frac38a',r'\ell_{\min}=\frac58a',r'a_0=\frac{3mg-mg}{m}=2g\quad\text{upwards}')+'<p>The upper stop occurs below the fixed end, so the separation stays positive. The string is slack between natural length and that stop; its remaining kinetic energy is converted into gravitational potential.</p>','slack_load',modulus=4,oldMass=3,remainingMass=1,naturalSpeed2='3/4',minimum='5/8',initialAcceleration=2),
 E('S2','2 · A string stretches on the other side of its anchor',r'<p>A particle moves on a smooth straight incline with sine \(\frac12\). A string of natural length a and modulus \(4mg\) joins it to O, which it may pass unobstructed. It starts at rest \(\frac32a\) uphill of O. Find the position and value of greatest speed on its first descent.</p>','<p>Let q be signed distance downhill from O.</p>'+D(r'z=\frac qa',r'p(z)=\max(|z|-1,0)',r'\frac{V(z)}{mga}=2p(z)^2-\frac z2',r'\frac{V_0}{mga}=\frac12+\frac34=\frac54',r'4(z-1)-\frac12=0,\qquad z=\frac98',r'\frac{V_{\min}}{mga}=-\frac{17}{32}',r'v_{\max}^2=\frac{57}{16}ag')+r'<p>Potential decreases through the uphill taut segment and the whole slack segment, then reaches its minimum 9a/8 downhill of O. The greatest speed is \(\frac{\sqrt{57}}4\sqrt{ag}\). Using a negative signed extension while passing O would give an incorrect spring model.</p>','cross_anchor',modulus=4,sine='1/2',uphill='3/2',position='9/8',speed2='57/16'),
 E('S3','3 · Momentum first, compression afterwards',r'<p>A particle of mass m strikes a stationary mass 2m attached to a horizontal guided spring at natural length. The incoming velocity towards the fixed end is \(3\sqrt{ag}\). They stick together. The spring has natural length a and modulus \(9mg\). Find the shared speed, greatest compression and kinetic energy lost in impact.</p>',D(r'm(3\sqrt{ag})=3mV',r'V=\sqrt{ag}',r'\frac12(3m)V^2=\frac{9mg}{2a}c^2',r'c=\frac a{\sqrt3}',r'K_{\rm lost}=\frac12m(3\sqrt{ag})^2-\frac12(3m)V^2',r'K_{\rm lost}=3mga')+'<p>The spring was initially unstressed, and the collision has no external impulse along the guide. Only the post-impact kinetic energy can be converted into elastic energy.</p>','coalesce',incomingMass=1,targetMass=2,incoming=3,modulus=9,shared=1,compression='1/sqrt(3)',lost=3),
 E('S4','4 · Entering a rough strip changes acceleration',r'<p>A mass m starts at natural length of a guided spring of length a and modulus \(4mg\), moving towards its fixed end at speed \(\sqrt{2ag}\). The table is smooth until compression reaches \(\frac a4\), then has sliding coefficient \(\frac12\). There is no step or impact. Find the speed squared and deceleration on each side of entry, then the greatest compression.</p>',D(r'v_C^2=2ag-4g\frac{(a/4)^2}{a}=\frac74ag',r'd_-=g,\qquad d_+=\frac32g',r'z=\frac ca,\qquad1=2z^2+\frac12\left(z-\frac14\right)',r'16z^2+4z-9=0',r'c=\frac{\sqrt{37}-1}{8}a')+'<p>Speed is continuous at C, but the friction adds g/2 to the deceleration. The selected root lies beyond a/4 and before compression a; the negative root is not on this path. The energy loss counts only the rough distance after C.</p>','rough_entry',modulus=4,initial2=2,entry='1/4',mu='1/2',entrySpeed2='7/4',smoothDeceleration=1,roughDeceleration='3/2',compression='(sqrt(37)-1)/8')])
